\details{
The function calculates metrics for the global network and can
optionally perform additional group-specific evaluations using
the same globally defined modules. Group-specific evaluations
recalculate connectivity and module membership within each
selected group without reconstructing the network or redefining
module assignments.

The following metrics are calculated for each gene:
\describe{
\item{kME (module membership)}{
Pearson correlation between gene expression and the eigengene
of its assigned module. The correlation method used during
network construction is retained for consistency.
}
\item{kWithin (intramodular connectivity)}{
Sum of adjacency values between a gene and the other genes
belonging to the same module.
}
\item{kOut (extramodular connectivity)}{
Sum of adjacency values between a gene and genes belonging
to other modules.
}
\item{kTotal (total connectivity)}{
Sum of intramodular and extramodular connectivity
(\code{kWithin + kOut}).
}
}

Module-level summaries include the number of genes assigned to
each module and the mean and median values of kME and kWithin.
Unassigned genes (grey module) are excluded from module-level
summaries. Their kME values are returned as \code{NA}, since
they do not belong to a defined module eigengene.

When group-specific evaluation is requested, the original global
module assignments and eigengenes are retained as the reference.
Gene expression and adjacency values are evaluated using only
samples belonging to each selected group. Consequently, kME
represents the correlation with the corresponding global module
eigengene evaluated within the selected group, while connectivity
metrics are recalculated using the group's expression data.

Group-specific metrics describe connectivity patterns within
each group and should not be interpreted as formal statistical
tests of differences between networks. Differences in connectivity
may also reflect differences in sample size and expression
variability.
